# T-2611: core.autocrlf=true (a Windows-checkout setting) is present on
# this Linux/WSL clone with no repo-wide normalization declared, so git
# silently writes CRLF into the working-tree copy of every tracked text
# file with no explicit `text`/`eol` attribute of its own -- confirmed on
# 60 of 60 sampled tracked src/frob/*.py files. Any measurement that reads
# the working tree byte-for-byte instead of going through a tool that
# already strips line endings (ruff, python's own line iteration, git
# itself) silently counts the trailing CR as content: this exact defect
# turned zero real E501 violations into four apparent ones in one session
# (T-2596). T-2586 already pinned `text eol=lf` for force-overrides.jsonl
# below (and, before T-2997 moved it out of git tracking, rapid-debt.jsonl)
# -- this generalizes that fix to the whole tree rather than replacing
# that line, which stays as an explicit, self-documenting pin even though
# this default now also covers it.
# `text=auto` normalizes CRLF to LF in the index for any file git detects
# as text (binary files are left alone); `eol=lf` pins the WORKING TREE
# to LF on checkout regardless of `core.autocrlf`, closing the actual
# hazard this ticket exists to fix. Declared here, in the tracked repo,
# rather than left to a per-clone `git config core.autocrlf=false`,
# because a config change does not travel with the repo and a fresh
# clone that skips it silently reproduces the same defect -- the same
# argument every other attribute in this file already makes for itself.
* text=auto eol=lf

# T-1258/T-2356: this repo cut over to ledger v2 (disjoint tickets/T-####/
# directories, ordinary git objects git's native per-file 3-way merge
# already resolves correctly). tickets.md/tickets-archive.md and the
# frob-ledger merge driver they needed are gone -- see
# docs/design/ledger-v2.md#7-reversible-migration-plan-design-for-the-child-ticket-not-built-here.
# The merge driver itself (`frob ticket merge-driver`, splice_ledger) is
# NOT removed from frob's own source -- other repos using frob may still
# be on v1/monofile mode during their own compatibility window; only
# THIS repo's now-inert data files and the .gitattributes lines that
# routed them through the driver are retired here.

# T-1873: force-overrides.jsonl is a tracked, append-only ledger -- a
# concurrent `--force` override appending near the same line hits git's
# default line-level merge, which reports a false textual conflict on a
# file whose whole value is that no record is ever lost. git's BUILT-IN
# `merge=union` driver is preferred over a new frob driver here: union is
# exactly append-only "keep both sides" semantics, and unlike `merge=
# frob-ledger` above it needs NO per-clone `git config` registration --
# a worktree that skipped that one-time setup silently falls back to the
# default (conflicting) driver, which is exactly the failure mode this
# ticket exists to close. Verified by reproduction (tests/unit/
# test_gitattributes_merge.py): two branches each appending a different
# record merge with both records surviving and zero conflict markers.
# T-0480: anchored with a leading slash for the same reason `/tickets.md`
# is above -- an unanchored pattern would also match any other file named
# `force-overrides.jsonl` anywhere in the tree. Covered here even though
# no `--force` override has happened yet in this repo (the file does not
# exist on disk), so the FIRST concurrent append is already covered
# rather than conflicting once, then getting a follow-up fix.
# T-2997: rapid-debt.jsonl used to be covered by an identical line here
# -- it moved to `.frob/rapid-debt.jsonl` (gitignored, per-checkout) and
# is no longer git-tracked at all, so it no longer needs a merge driver;
# a per-checkout file is never merged in the first place.
/force-overrides.jsonl merge=union

# T-2586: this repo's own `core.autocrlf=true` (a Windows-checkout
# setting, present on this Linux/WSL clone) converts LF to CRLF on
# checkout for any tracked text file with no explicit `text`/`eol`
# attribute -- `git check-attr text eol -- force-overrides.jsonl` read
# "unspecified" before this line. `eol=lf` pins this file to LF on
# checkout AND normalizes CRLF to LF on commit, matching the `merge=
# union` line immediately above -- both attributes are declared HERE, in
# the tracked repo, rather than left to a per-clone `git config core.
# autocrlf=false`, because a config change does not travel with the repo
# and a clone that skips it silently falls back to the broken behavior
# (the same argument that chose a built-in merge driver over a per-clone
# git config for the `merge=union` line above).
# T-2997: rapid-debt.jsonl's identical line was removed alongside its
# `merge=union` line above -- see that note.
/force-overrides.jsonl text eol=lf

# T-1433 incident: attachment shas are byte-exact; CRLF conversion breaks COV004.
# T-2239: T-1433's glob only matched the OLD v1 flat layout (tickets/attachments/**).
# Ledger v2 stores attachments per-ticket (tickets/<id>/attachments/**), a path shape
# the v1 glob never matches, so v2 attachments were still CRLF-converted on checkout,
# desyncing their on-disk sha256 from the sha256 recorded at attach time (LF content).
# Both layouts are covered here -- v1 attachments still exist on disk (tickets/
# attachments/T-1433/...) and must not regress.
tickets/attachments/** -text
tickets/*/attachments/** -text
